 "comment": "A firm's s.194C payees, each priced at the section's lower rate (1%) and higher rate (2%): 12 rows, 8 findings. Alder Carriers, classified one-off: c01 (Rs 30,000.01) is over the single-sum limit and is its own tranche on 10 May; c02 is exactly Rs 30,000 and waits; after c04 the year's total is exactly Rs 1,00,000, which is not over; c05 (1 paisa) takes it over on 25 September, so c02 to c05 (Rs 70,000) are one tranche dated 25 September; c06 (Rs 4,100), after the crossing, is its own tranche on 5 November; c07 debits Alder Carriers Rs 6,000 against the expense, a reversal that lowers nothing. Being one-off, Alder Carriers adds the three one-off totals. Birch Haulage, classified foreseeable: no credit is over the single-sum limit; its credits are read in date order, not book order: c08, dated 31 March and listed first, is the last of them and takes the total to Rs 1,16,599.99, so c08 to c11 are one tranche dated 31 March, whose own-date maximum runs c09 from 30 April (18 months), c10 from 31 December (10), c11 from 1 January (9) and c08 from 31 March (7). Cedar Cartage is two ledgers the configuration names as one payee, not classified: neither ledger alone passes Rs 1,00,000, the two together pass it on c15 (2 August), so c12 to c15 are one tranche dated 2 August; the foreseeability list also names the ledger Cedar Cartage North, which is not a payee entity and classifies nothing. c16 pays Rs 45,000 from the bank with no payee: tds_payees lists it as payee not named, and it is priced like any other payee, on 3 October, not classified.",
